      en: "Shiretoko National Park is a remarkable feature of the geography and culture of Japan. One of Japan's most remote and unspoiled parks. The Shiretoko Peninsula is famous for its large brown bear population and rich marine life. A UNESCO World Natural Heritage site. One of the world's densest brown bear populations. Visitors can often spot bears from tour boats along the coast. This protected area is part of Japan's rich wildlife and flora, from the subtropical forests of Kyushu to the snowy regions of Hokkaido. Like many notable places in Japan, it combines regional character with the legacy of the samurai tradition, Shinto and Buddhist heritage, and the modern industrial nation. Thus, this place contributes to the diverse profile of Japan — from Mount Fuji and Tokyo to the volcanic island arc along the Pacific.",
